  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\nA cold front that dropped through the area into central Kentucky\nearly this morning will eventually move back through the area today\nas a warm front. This will occur as an area of low pressure ejects\nnortheastward out of the central Plains into the lower Midwest and\nGreat Lakes by the evening. \n\nFrom the mid morning into the early afternoon, the incoming low\npressure will transition our winds from northeasterly to\nsoutheasterly. Plentiful moisture remains trapped within the\ntransition, leading mostly cloudy to overcast conditions through much\nof the day, including the afternoon. \n\nAs the warm front lifts northward late in the afternoon and evening,\nthe local area will be within the warm sector of the incoming system\nwith the cold front still off to the west. A few showers are \nexpected along the warm front as it lifts north, but the primary \nsevere threat will exist with any thunderstorms that are able to form\nsouth of the warm front in the better heating. While forecast \nsoundings do indicate favorable turning of the winds (resulting in \ncurved hodographs) in the lower parts of the atmosphere, extensive \ncloud cover prevents more supportive surface temperatures. The lack \nof better heating results in a low-level inversion that is maintained\nthrough much of the afternoon and into the early evening. As \ninstability begins to become more favorable, the directional wind \nshear is much less curved, keeping any tornado threat very limited. \nInstead, a few storms capable of damaging winds is the primary \nconcern during the evening hours. \n\nWith the deep layer winds becoming more unidirectional, multiple\nrounds of storms impacting the same areas could eventually lead to \nswaths of locally heavy rainfall. The best chance for this to happen \nis across portions of eastern Indiana and west-central Ohio. \nOtherwise, most area may remain rainfall free until the cold front \napproaches Friday morning. \n\nKey Message 2)\nExpectations remain on track for the low pressure to intensity as it\nmoves through the Great Lakes Thursday night into Friday. The \nassociated cold front moves from west to east Friday morning. That \nmeans the warmest temperatures in the mid to upper 60s will occur \nduring the early morning hours through daybreak on Friday. Winds will\nshift out of the northwest, gusting up to 30 mph Friday morning and \nearly afternoon. Along and behind the front, additional rain showers \nand periods of late rain are expected as the main forcing associated \nwith the trough moves through the region. Temperatures in the mid to \nupper 50s are forecast the rest of the day. With the combination of \nwind, rain, and dropping temperatures, conditions will feel quite \ndifferent from the early morning bus pickup to the afternoon drop \noff, especially across central and southern Ohio. \n\nWinds begin to taper off late Friday night into Saturday morning as\nhigh pressure builds in from the west, but it likely will not be fast\nenough to result in frost Saturday morning. Instead, the most\nfavorable time for frost will be Saturday night into Sunday morning\nas high pressure shifts eastward across the region. Temperatures\nquickly drop into the lower 40s and upper 30s Saturday evening, with\nsome locations dropping below freezing by daybreak Sunday.",
